# Messages of lib/checks/85-backup.sh. Format and rules: see common.lang.

section.85-backup=Backups

# ---- BAK-STATUS ----
BAK-STATUS.none.title=No backup tool detected
BAK-STATUS.none.impact=No known backup tool is installed, and no scheduled job copies files. Limit of this check: a backup to a NAS, to an online service or a manual copy to an external disk leaves no trace here. If you have none of these, a disk failure, a theft or a mistaken deletion means losing your files for good.
BAK-STATUS.none.act.1=If you have no backup, set one up: Déjà Dup (the "Backups" application of Ubuntu) copies your personal folder to an external disk, a network folder or an online service, on a schedule.
BAK-STATUS.info.title=Backup tools detected: {tools}
BAK-STATUS.info.impact=Inventory of the backup tools and scheduled copies found here, with the date of the last backup when it can be read locally. A snapshot kept on the same disk as the system (Timeshift, snapper) protects against a bad update, not against the failure or theft of that disk. Limit: a backup to a NAS, an online service or a manual copy leaves no trace here. The audit reports a scheduled backup older than {days} days.
BAK-STATUS.warn.title=Scheduled backup late or failed: {tools}
BAK-STATUS.warn.impact=A backup that is meant to run on a schedule has not succeeded for more than {days} days, or its last scheduled run failed. Files created or changed since the last good backup would be lost with the disk.
BAK-STATUS.warn.act.1=Open the backup tool, look at its last error and run a backup by hand; check that it completes.
BAK-STATUS.warn.act.2=Lists the scheduled tasks (timers) with their last and next run. Read-only.
BAK-STATUS.warn.act.3=Shows the last messages of the backup service named in the evidence (add --user for a timer of your session). Read-only.
BAK-STATUS.warn.act.4=For a backup to an external disk or a NAS, check that the destination is connected and has free space.
BAK-STATUS.warn.caution=A backup is only proven by a restore: after fixing it, restore one file to another folder and open it.
